why 1 1/2 DOUBLING Soprano (A–B) and tenor (D–B) both arrive on B; the tenor moves on to A at once, so the doubling lasts an eighth.
why 1 3/4 DOUBLING Alto and bass both have F♯ in V⁶. The bass's F♯ rises to G at once, as it should; the alto's belongs to its own line and falls back to E.
why 3 1/4 DOUBLING Soprano (C–B) and tenor (A–B) meet on B by step from opposite sides.
why 4 0 RANGE The bass leaps up to D and then drops an octave to D for the cadence; the high D lasts a beat.
why 5 0 RANGE The bass leaps up an octave to D and walks down D–C–B–A: a short high start for the phrase.
why 6 0 TENDENCY The alto holds A into the next chord and then falls to G♯: it resolves down by step, one beat later.
why 7 3/4 SIMILAR_MOTION Everything rises toward B minor: the bass's A♯ and the tenor's C♯ are leading tones, and the tune climbs to E. Each line has its own reason to rise.
why 8 0 TENDENCY The tune holds E into the next chord and steps down to D♯ a beat later. It does fall by step, after a delay, as the melody wants.
why 8 3/4 RANGE The new phrase starts with the bass high on D, walking down D–C–B–A. Starting high gives it room to descend.
why 9 1/4 PARALLEL_OCTAVES Soprano and alto go from an octave G to a unison B, with the alto's A in between. The tune leaps down a sixth, so the two voices meet; Bach accepts this, strict exercises do not.
why 9 1/4 DOUBLING The tune leaps down to B and the alto steps A–B, so both share B for a beat. The tune decides the soprano's note; the alto takes the nearest one.
why 9 1/2 SPACING The tenor drops an octave to G while the alto holds a high B; it climbs back A–B over the next eighths.
why 9 3/4 DOUBLING Soprano (C–B) and tenor (A–B) meet on B by step.
why 11 0 DOUBLING Soprano (D–E) and alto (F♯–E) meet on E from opposite sides, while the tenor's D is a suspension falling to C.
why 12 1/2 LEADING_TONE The tenor's F♯ leaps up to B, the third of the final G chord. Rising to G would leave the chord without its third.
why 7 0 SEVENTH The tenor's A rises to B while the bass takes G, the note A would fall to. The resolution moves to the bass, and the tenor climbs on toward C and D.
